From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 08:23:43 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: samsung: Add the Exynos9820 ABOX driver

The vendor kernel hands every DMA and mixer operation of ABOX to firmware
on the DSP core of the block, and only reads the buffer position back.
This driver leaves that core off and programs the same registers from
the application processor:

  UAIF1 playback: RDMA0 -> SPUS input 0 -> SIFS1 -> UAIF1
  UAIF1 capture:  UAIF1 -> NSRC0 -> SPUM -> SIFM0 -> WDMA1
  UAIF0 playback: RDMA1 -> SPUS input 1 -> SIFS2 -> UAIF0
  UAIF0 capture:  UAIF0 -> NSRC1 -> SPUM -> SIFM1 -> WDMA2

The layout of the DMA control register (format, burst length, buffer
update, enable) and the order the fields are written in come from the
accessor functions of the vendor firmware; the RDMA and WDMA engines
share it. The routing values come from the mixer controls of the vendor
driver: NSRC0 takes UAIF1 as input 9, and SIFMn feeds WDMA(n+1), WDMA0
being fed by the recording path instead; the SPUS output code picks the
SIFS an input goes to (0 is SIFS1, 2 is SIFS2), and UAIFn takes SIFSk as
the value k + 1. The shared bank memory of NSRC0 is placed where the
firmware places it, 512 bytes from byte 0x400; the vendor driver sets
only the sizes, so RDMA1 and NSRC1 go right after RDMA0 and NSRC0.

UAIF pulls the samples: an RDMA engine with nothing pulling on the other
end of the chain does not move, so the bit clock of the port starts with
the first prepared stream and stops with the last freed one. That order
also suits the amplifiers on this port, whose PLLs take their reference
from the bit clock and which DAPM powers up after the component has been
prepared.

The DMA interrupts go to the interrupt controller inside the block, which
only the DSP core listens to, so period boundaries are timed with an
hrtimer and the position is read from the status register of the engine.

PLL_AUD0 is fractional and comes out at 1179648010 Hz, so the divider
that gives a 1.536 MHz bit clock lands 0.013 Hz above it, and a divider
rounding down skips to the next value, 0.26 % slow. The bit clock is
asked for with 100 ppm of headroom and accepted within 100 ppm.

Only 16 bit stereo at 48 kHz is supported, the one format measured on
hardware: the position advances by 193 bytes per millisecond
against 192 expected, and both directions run together.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
